N('fort-stevens-battery-russell','Fort Stevens and Battery Russell','Warrenton, Oregon','or','fort','standing',46.19247,-123.97538,'site','public','drive','','1863–1947',
  'A fort at the south side of the mouth of the Columbia River. It began in 1863 and 1864 as an earthwork battery, was named Fort Stevens in 1865 for the dead Washington territorial governor Isaac Stevens, and was the main post of the three-fort defence of the river mouth. The army kept it until 1947. On the night of June 21 to 22, 1942, the Japanese submarine I-25 fired 17 shells from its deck gun at it, the first attack on a military post in the contiguous United States in the war; Wikipedia says the only thing destroyed was the backstop of the baseball field, and that one shell fell near Battery Russell. The armaments were removed and the buildings auctioned after 1947; the ground passed to the state in 1975.',
  'Concrete gun batteries, bunkers and the old parade ground inside a large state park. The pin is on Battery Russell, which Wikipedia says visitors can still reach. The state park page says there is a military museum with year-round displays and, in summer, underground tours of a gun battery that served as a World War II command centre (the park does not say which battery). The wreck of the sailing ship Peter Iredale, 1906, lies on the beach in the same park.',
  'A Civil War fort whose concrete batteries were shelled by a Japanese submarine in 1942.',
  'Fort Stevens State Park, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, open for day use all year; a day-use parking permit is bought on site (the park page). Park at the lot on the Fort Stevens Ridge Trail and walk. For the summer battery tours call the Friends of Old Fort Stevens at 503-861-2000 (park page).',
  [W('Fort Stevens (Oregon)'),W('Bombardment of Fort Stevens'),['Oregon State Parks: Fort Stevens',OP%'129']],
  conf='mixed',th=[46.19365,-123.97481],thn='Fort Stevens Ridge Trail lot',brick='no'),
N('umpqua-river-lighthouse','Umpqua River Lighthouse','Winchester Bay, Oregon','or','light','standing',43.66229,-124.19848,'exact','open','drive','','1894 (or 1890)',
  'The first light here, built at the river mouth in 1855 and lit in 1857, had its foundations undermined by the river and collapsed in October 1863. Congress paid for a new one in 1888. Wikipedia says building began in 1892 and the light was first lit in 1894, from the same plans as Heceta Head; the museum that runs the tower says it was built in 1890. The tower stands about 100 feet above the river. Its clockwork turning gear was replaced when the light was automated in 1966, then put back in 1985 after a public outcry. The lighthouse was listed on the National Register in 1977.',
  'The 65-foot tower (museum figure) with its first-order Fresnel lens, two tons of 616 prisms made in France, shown on a guided climb. A coastal history museum stands beside it. The stairs are not wheelchair accessible.',
  'A river-mouth tower whose first lighthouse fell into the river.',
  'Umpqua Lighthouse Museum, run by the Douglas County Museum Foundation, 1020 Lighthouse Road, Winchester Bay; call 541-271-4631 to book a tour. The state park page says the tower is half a mile from Umpqua Lighthouse State Park. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Umpqua River Light'),['Umpqua Valley Museums: lighthouse tours','https://umpquavalleymuseums.org/visit/umpqua-lighthouse-museum/lighthouse-tours/'],['Oregon State Parks: Umpqua Lighthouse',OP%'83']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Tours daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. (October to February), to 6 p.m. (March to September); call to book'),
N('cape-blanco-lighthouse','Cape Blanco Lighthouse','Sixes, Oregon','or','light','standing',42.83667,-124.56333,'exact','open','drive','','1870',
  'A lighthouse on the most westerly point of Oregon, built under Lt. Col. Robert Williamson and lit in 1870 on land bought from John and Mary West in 1867. Bricks were made on site; the rancher Patrick Hughes gave access to the clay. The keepers James Langlois (42 years) and James Hughes (38 years) are the best known. The head keepers\' quarters were built in 1909 after years of asking for more room. It is on the National Register.',
  'The tower and keepers\' quarters on the cape. The Hughes family house, built by James Hughes across the Sixes River, is also open to tours. What colour the tower is and what the interior holds were not checked.',
  'The westernmost lighthouse in Oregon, built of bricks made on the cape.',
  'Cape Blanco State Park, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, with the tower managed by the Bureau of Land Management; its page (blm.gov) could not be opened when this was written. A search listing from a Bandon directory gave tours Wednesday to Monday, 10 a.m. to 3:30 p.m., April to October, closed Tuesdays; a 2023 notice said the season began a month late that year. Hours not checked at the source.',
  [W('Cape Blanco Light'),['Bureau of Land Management: Cape Blanco Lighthouse','https://www.blm.gov/visit/cape-blanco-lighthouse-nhs'],['Oregon State Parks: Cape Blanco',OP%'44']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('tillamook-hangar-b','Hangar B, Tillamook Air Museum','Tillamook, Oregon','or','industry','standing',45.42039,-123.80483,'exact','open','drive','','1943',
  'A wooden hangar built by the Navy for the K-class blimps of Naval Air Station Tillamook, which opened in December 1942 to patrol the coast and escort convoys. Hangar B was finished in August 1943; its twin, Hangar A, was built in 27 working days and burned down on August 22, 1992. The station was closed in 1948. The museum began in 1992. An engineering report in 2011 found the structure in worse shape than thought (Wikipedia).',
  'A building 1,072 feet long, 296 feet wide and 192 feet high on over seven acres, held up by more than 3.2 million board feet of Pacific Northwest lumber, with six doors 120 feet tall and 30 tons each and two catwalks 137 feet up. Wikipedia calls it the largest clear-span wooden structure in the world. It now holds an aircraft museum.',
  'The biggest wooden room in the world, built to hold six blimps.',
  'Tillamook Air Museum, south of Tillamook off Highway 101; the museum page gives directions but no opening days or hours when opened, so hours are not checked. Admission not checked.',
  [W('Tillamook Air Museum'),['Tillamook Air Museum: Hangar B','https://tillamookair.com/hangar-b']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('oregon-caves-chateau','Oregon Caves Chateau','Cave Junction, Oregon','or','hotel','standing',42.09841,-123.40757,'site','view','drive','','1934',
  'A six-story rustic hotel at the Oregon Caves, designed and built by the local contractor Gust Lium in 1934 to the vision of the landscape architect Arthur Peck. It was made a National Historic Landmark in October 1987 for its design. It is closed for a large rehabilitation of fire safety, accessibility and earthquake strengthening; the National Park Service says the original contract was ended in March 2023 for lack of money and the next step was being weighed.',
  'The exterior is sheathed in bark (Wikipedia says redwood bark, the Park Service says Port Orford cedar bark), over a steep ravine so that three storeys at the front are six from the creek. The Park Service says marble from the cave was used in the stonework, and that the Chateau holds the largest publicly owned collection of Monterey furniture. The dining room, coffee shop and rooms are shut.',
  'A bark-covered 1934 chateau built across a ravine, shut for repairs.',
  'Oregon Caves National Monument and Preserve, National Park Service. The Chateau is closed until further notice; look at it from outside. Cave tours, guided only, run in spring, summer and fall from a separate entrance (park hours page).',
  [W('Oregon Caves Chateau'),['National Park Service: the Chateau','https://www.nps.gov/orca/learn/historyculture/chateau.htm'],['National Park Service: Oregon Caves hours','https://www.nps.gov/orca/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('crater-lake-lodge','Crater Lake Lodge','Crater Lake, Oregon','or','hotel','standing',42.90972,-122.14083,'exact','open','drive','','1915; rebuilt 1990s',
  'A hotel on the rim of the caldera, nearly 1,000 feet above the lake, begun in 1909 and first opened in 1915. It was saved from demolition and in 1995 rebuilt and stabilised against earthquakes (National Park Service); another Park Service page gives work in 1988 and 1994, and says most of the materials in the present lodge are modern. The Rim Village, of which it is one of six main buildings, was listed as a historic district in 1997.',
  'A large lodge on the edge of the caldera with a restaurant and a lounge. How much of the 1915 fabric survives was not checked; the sources suggest little of the structure itself.',
  'A lodge on the rim of Crater Lake, rebuilt behind its 1915 look.',
  'Crater Lake National Park, owned by the National Park Service and run by the concessioner ExplorCraterLake. Typically open mid-May to early October. Rooms and entry fees at the park; lodge hours not checked.',
  [W('Crater Lake Lodge'),['National Park Service: Crater Lake Lodge','https://www.nps.gov/places/crater-lake-lodge.htm'],['National Park Service: lodging','https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/craterlakelodge.htm']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Mid-May to early October (lodge season); front desk and dining hours not given'),
N('timberline-lodge','Timberline Lodge','Government Camp, Oregon','or','hotel','standing',45.33126,-121.71137,'exact','open','drive','','1936–1938',
  'A mountain lodge on the south side of Mount Hood built by the Works Progress Administration from 1936 to 1938 and furnished by local craftspeople. President Franklin Roosevelt dedicated it on September 28, 1937. It was handed to the Forest Service in January 1938 and opened to guests in early February 1938. It is a National Historic Landmark in the Mount Hood National Forest.',
  'A four-storey lodge of about 40,000 square feet, with ground-floor walls of rubble masonry from local boulders and heavy timber above. The central head house is a hexagon 60 feet across with a six-sided stone chimney 90 feet high and 14 feet across, each side with a fireplace. The 1930s hand-made ironwork, carving and furniture are the reason to go in; a search result for its own site listed free Forest Service tours Thursday to Monday, but that page could not be opened here.',
  'A Depression-era lodge on Mount Hood built and furnished by hand.',
  'Mount Hood National Forest, publicly owned and privately run, on the Mount Hood Scenic Byway at 6,000 feet. Public areas are open to visitors; opening times and the tour days were not confirmed from its own pages. Winter parking can be full.',
  [W('Timberline Lodge'),['Timberline Lodge','https://www.timberlinelodge.com/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('pittock-mansion','Pittock Mansion','Portland, Oregon','or','landmark','standing',45.52516,-122.71611,'site','open','drive','','1909–1914',
  'A French Renaissance style house built from 1909 to 1914 for Henry Pittock, the London-born publisher of The Oregonian, and his wife Georgiana, designed by the San Francisco architect Edward Foulkes. The city owns it and it has been a museum; it was listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'Forty-six rooms of Tenino sandstone on 46 acres in the West Hills, with a central vacuum, intercoms, indirect lighting, an elevator and a walk-in refrigerator when new. The rooms follow different styles (a Jacobean library, a Turkish smoking room, a French music room). The Gate Lodge is also open.',
  'A 1914 hilltop mansion with an elevator and a walk-in refrigerator.',
  'Pittock Mansion, 3229 NW Pittock Drive, owned by the City of Portland and run by Pittock Mansion. Admission is charged for the mansion; the main lot is paid parking (the museum page), and hikers can use free gravel pullouts on Pittock Drive. Fall and winter hours are on the page.',
  [W('Pittock Mansion'),['Pittock Mansion: plan your visit','https://www.pittockmansion.org/visit']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., Tuesdays noon to 4; last entry 30 minutes before closing; closed some holidays and in January'),
N('chambers-railroad-bridge','Chambers Covered Railroad Bridge','Cottage Grove, Oregon','or','rail','standing',43.78936,-123.06962,'exact','public','walk','','1925; rebuilt 2010–2011',
  'A covered bridge of Howe truss, 78 feet long over the Coast Fork Willamette, built in 1925 to carry logs from the Lorane Valley to the Chambers sawmill on the far bank. The mill burned a second time and closed in the 1950s, and the rails were taken up. Wikipedia calls it the only fully covered railroad bridge left west of the Mississippi; the City of Cottage Grove calls it the only one in Oregon. The sources disagree on its date: one 1976 National Park Service form gives 1936. It was taken down in 2010 and replaced by a copy, finished in November 2011 with a federal covered-bridge grant of $1,315,370.',
  'A boxed wooden bridge now used by walkers and cyclists. Because it was rebuilt, what stands is a reconstruction on the original design, not the 1925 timber.',
  'A covered bridge built for log trains, now for walkers.',
  'City of Cottage Grove, open to people on foot and bicycle (the city and a regional tourism page). It is near S River Road and the Riverwalk housing; where best to park was not checked.',
  [W('Chambers Covered Bridge'),['City of Cottage Grove: Chambers Railroad Bridge','https://cottagegroveor.gov/community/page/chambers-railroad-bridge']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('butteville-store','Historic Butteville Store','Butteville, Oregon','or','landmark','standing',45.26198,-122.84194,'exact','open','drive','','1863',
  'A country store at the old river landing of Butteville, which was founded in the 1840s by Methodist Mission people and rivalled Champoeg for shipping the French Prairie crops in the 1850s. Several sources give 1863 for the store. The Friends of Historic Butteville say it stands on the land claim of Alexis Aubichon, probably filed in 1851. A 2006 state parks study found it the oldest continuously operating retail business in Oregon, though the Oregon Encyclopedia says it was among, not the first, of the town\'s trading posts and is the only one left.',
  'The store, which still serves ice cream, sandwiches and drinks and has local history on show; the Butteville Community Church is the only other building left of the old town (Oregon Encyclopedia). Original fittings not checked.',
  'The oldest store still trading in Oregon, on a river landing that lost its boats.',
  'Run by the Friends of Historic Butteville beside Champoeg State Heritage Area. Hours were not given on the pages read; see butteville.org. The store is at 10767 Butte Street NE.',
  [W('Butteville, Oregon'),['Oregon Encyclopedia: Butteville Store','https://www.oregonencyclopedia.org/articles/butteville-store/'],['Friends of Historic Butteville','https://butteville.org/butteville_store.html']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
